{"status":"ok","message-type":"work","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,10]],"date-time":"2026-10-10T16:00:41Z","timestamp":1791648041754,"version":"4.3.4"},"edition-number":"1","reference-count":10,"publisher":"Wiley","isbn-type":[{"value":"9781405196949","type":"print"},{"value":"9781118663219","type":"electronic"}],"license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2016,4,21]],"date-time":"2016-04-21T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1461196800000},"content-version":"tdm","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"http:\/\/doi.wiley.com\/10.1002\/tdm_license_1.1"}],"content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"short-container-title":[],"abstract":"<jats:p>Social role theory is a social psychological theory that pertains to sex differences and similarities in social behavior. Its key principle is that differences and similarities arise primarily from the distribution of men and women into social roles within their society. Through socialization and the formation of gender roles, the behaviors of men and women generally support and sustain the division of labor. In industrialized economies, for example, social roles are organized so that women are more likely than men to be homemakers and primary caretakers of children and to hold caretaking jobs in the paid economy. In contrast, men are more likely than women to be primary family providers and to assume full\u2010time roles in the paid economy, often ones that involve physical strength, assertiveness, or leadership skills.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1002\/9781118663219.wbegss183","type":"other","created":{"date-parts":[[2016,4,21]],"date-time":"2016-04-21T20:25:27Z","timestamp":1461270327000},"page":"1-3","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":300,"title":["Social Role Theory of Sex Differences"],"prefix":"10.1002","author":[{"given":"Alice H.","family":"Eagly","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Wendy","family":"Wood","sequence":"additional","a